Less than 24 hours until the legislative session kicks off. Can you feel the excitement?! No? Well, you're probably not in Tallahassee then. Let's have a look at what's at stake in the 2018 legislative session.
Here we go: Every year, right before session time, reporters who cover the Legislature offer their previews of what voters can expect out of this year's hoopla. And while the issues may be different, like sea turtles returning to South Florida, we again have our preview stories. The Orlando Sentinel's Gray Rohrer reports that political ambitions and open seats due to scandals, death and appointments to higher office are making for a particularly volatile year.
As for the key issues that will likely be tackled over the next two months, I've got a rundown of all of them. You can expect some of this stuff to move out of one chamber or the other this week as both the House and Senate move forward with their priorities. But those priorities are not the same, so don't expect Gov. Rick Scott to sign anything for a while.
Water wars: The years-long battle between Florida and Georgia over water usage is headed to the U.S. Supreme Court, the Associated Press's Gary Fineout and Mark Sherman report. At stake is the flow of water out of Georgia and into Florida via the Apalachicola River, and the hard-hit oyster industry around Apalachicola Bay, where the river dumps out into the Gulf of Mexico. That industry has seen fewer and fewer oysters over the years, and while the reasons are many, one of the primary ones cited by Florida officials is the overuse of water in Georgia, or as U.S. Sen. Marco Rubio put it in an email this morning, "Georgia��s gratuitous overconsumption of water." Rubio has pushed for millions of dollars in disaster relief funding for the area over the past half decade.
Incidentally, if you missed the Sun Sentinel's Emmy Award-winning expose on the Gulf oyster industry and the health hazards of eating raw oysters farmed there, you really must check it out. (Yes, our newspaper won an Emmy. No, I didn't think newspapers could do that either. But the photo/video is really, really good.)
Quick, to the talk shows! You may have heard there is a certain book out which paints President Donald Trump in an extremely buffoonish light. Few people have done more for sales of Michael Wolff's "Fire and Fury" than Trump himself, who has spent the past several days relentlessly attacking the book and its author, giving the tome the sort of publicity of which even the best ad campaigns could only dream. Yesterday, Trump's loyalists in the White House (the ones who didn't dog the man in Wolff's book) went on the political talk shows to defend the president, the Associated Press's Jill Colvin reports. Former White House senior strategist Steve Bannon also walked back some of the comments he made in the book, explaining that when he called a meeting between Russians and Paul Manafort, Donald Trump Jr. and Jared Kushner "treasonous," he was referring to Manafort alone, not Trump Jr. Unclear what he thought Trump Jr. was doing there. The elder Trump also countered the book's depiction of him as a possibly unhinged, definitely incompetent cretin by tweeting that his "two greatest assets have been mental stability and being, like, really smart" and that he is "a very stable genius."
Business as usual: In the sort of message discipline the Trump administration has become famous for, President Donald Trump on Friday at Camp David expressed skepticism that public-private partnerships would work as the centerpiece of his $1 trillion infrastructure plan, which is still under development. The next day, economic adviser Gary Cohn presented to Republican leaders a broad blueprint of an infrastructure plan, the centerpiece of which was $200 billion in federal funds for public-private partnerships, the Washington Post's Josh Dawsey reports.
Out: The Sun Sentinel's Anthony Man reports that Broward Republican Party chairman Bob Sutton has resigned, capping off (or at least providing another notable milestone in) months of bitter infighting.
In... well, in Palm Beach, anyway. Given the latest FAA advisories, it's likely that President Donald Trump will return to Mar-a-Lago this weekend, the Sun Sentinel's Aric Chokey reports.
